ming until  summer or even later then what does this  mean for the regular 27-inch high-end  imac because as we know the current  intel imac starts at 1800  and the old imac pro used to start at 5  000  so if there is only going to be one  high-end imac pro coming out later this  year with a high starting price of  around four thousand dollars or higher  then there would be a massive price gap  between the 24 inch m1 imac for thirteen  hundred dollars and this new very  expensive imac pro and because of this i  think apple absolutely needs to have a  27 inch imac at a starting price of  around 1800 or 2 000  no matter what so what i'm gonna do in  this video is try to make sense of all  the different ways we could still have a  consumer friendly lower priced 27 inch  imac while also getting an additional  very high end imac pro and in my opinion  there are three main scenarios for how  apple can make this happen so let's go  through all of them for the first  scenario i can see apple completely  renaming the larger 27-inch imac as the  new imac pro so that it's more simple  and easy to understand and differentiate  between the smaller 24-inch imac for  consumers and the larger imac pro for  pros so they would essentially reveal  just a single imac pro later on this  summer and it would start at around two  thousand dollars and come with  essentially the same m1 pro chip as you  get on the 16 inch macbook pro but then  for the upgrade options you'll of course  get to choose the m1 max and then most  likely a new higher end chip like either  the m1 max duo like we've been talking  about for a while  which would include apple taking two m1  max dies and combining them together  using the secret die to die interconnect  that they conveniently left out of their  marketing images or on the other hand it  could actually come with the new  mysterious 12 core chip that dylan  leaked last week and those upgrades  would get very expensive probably around  four or five thousand dollars before  even going crazy on upgrading the ram  and storage so in this scenario instead  of the past when apple had both a  27-inch imac and a 27-inch imac pro  they could both be built into one  machine just with a very wide range of  pricing and this could actually make  sense the reason for that is because  mark german believes there's a good  chance that apple could reveal the apple  silicon mac pro at this year's wwdc in  june just so that they can say that they  finished the two-year transition on time  and this idea makes sense because wwdc  was actually when they revealed the  previous mac pro and they just delayed  the launch until december now in my mind  this could actually make a ton of sense  because the mac pro is expected to come  with both the m1 max duo chip and the m1  max quadro so i can see them revealing  those chips and then announcing that the  new imac pro will come with up to a 20  core m1 max duo but if you want the 40  core quadro you've got to go with the  mac pro and this would also make it a  little bit easier in terms of marketing  those chips meant for the most demanding  workloads out there but now let's move  on to the second scenario and this one  is actually quite similar to the first  but with a small little tweak what if  instead of apple just releasing one imac  pro in the summer with a wide range of  chip options there's actually going to  be two separate products revealed at  different price points a regular 27 inch  imac and then an imac pro just like it  was in the past so the 27 inch imac  would come with an m1 pro chip up to the  same m1 max configuration available in  the new macbook pros and then if you  wanted a more powerful chip you'd have  to go with the imac pro with either that  new 12 core chip or the m1 max duo and  the reason it makes sense to split them  up into two products is because it'll be  more clear and easy for people to  understand the price points with apple  giving a certain starting price for the  imac pro and if you think about it the  previous imac pro was known to be very  expensive starting at five thousand  dollars so if apple had to combine them  into one imac pro product costing as low  as two thousand dollars it would lose  that high-end premium professional feel  and then on top of that it would be very  difficult to know how much performance a  certain imac pro has just by looking at  it for example you'd have no idea if  someone's imac pro was the two thousand  dollar base model or a very expensive  five thousand dollar plus model with an  m1 max duo and this could cause some  confusion so this scenario would  basically mean that later on this summer  apple would reveal both a 27-ish imac  and an imac pro but now we have the  third scenario which is by far my  favorite so let's get right into it when  ross young tweeted about the imac pro  delay i decided to ask him if there's a  chance that there could be a regular  27-inch imac launching this spring and  then an imac pro later and he replied by  saying that he's not sure because  they're only tracking the one imac pro  product so because of this there's still  a good chance that we could get both  products not just one and if you think  about it this makes the most sense  because it would allow apple to fill the  price gap between the 24-inch imac and  the imac pro with a regular  consumer-focused 27-inch model starting  at around 1800 or 2 000  which could be revealed at apple's  spring event and if you're doubtful of  that happening it was recently revealed  that the current nano texture display  option on the intel imac now has a  shipping date for the end of march which  is pretty odd unless a new 27-inch imac  actually comes at apple spring event  which i think will be happening in march  and i really think this would make sense  because they'd be able to have a much  more consumer-focused imac with a larger  display and up to an m1 max chip just  like the macbook pros and of course 